# --- Never-publish boundary -------------------------------------------------------
# The control was real (this file's export-ignore list) with no enforcement behind it:
# `git archive HEAD` with no path args shipped every path below until this line. Every
# top-level path this repo tracks was enumerated and ruled on, one line of reasoning
# each -- internal planning, financial, and analyst-process documents never shipped to
# a stranger's clone, everything else (product code, tests, this repo's own dogfooding
# config) shipped as normal. `tests/unit/test_archive_boundary.py` holds the executable
# proof: it re-derives its own independent list of what should be excluded (not by
# trusting this file's say-so) and asserts a real `git archive HEAD` run contains none
# of it, plus a closed-world check that a future untracked-and-unruled path fails loud
# rather than shipping by default. This file is itself tracked and NOT export-ignored —
# the policy has to ship with the archive it governs, or a fresh clone has no way to
# inherit it.
#
# Gotcha, found live and worth not re-discovering: a `.gitattributes` line splits on
# whitespace into `pattern attr1 attr2 ...`, so a filename containing a literal space
# ("Revenue forecast.txt" below) cannot be a plain unquoted pattern token -- it silently
# parses as pattern "Revenue" plus two bogus attributes, and the file still ships,
# despite the export-ignore line being present and looking correct. Backslash-escaping
# the space (`Revenue\ forecast.txt`) does NOT fix this either -- confirmed directly via
# `git check-attr export-ignore -- "Revenue forecast.txt"` still returning `unspecified`.
# The only form that actually works is C-quoting the whole pattern
# ("Revenue forecast.txt" export-ignore, as below) -- confirmed via `git check-attr`
# returning `set`, and via a real `git archive HEAD` run no longer containing the file.
# If a future export-ignore line looks present but the archive still ships the file,
# re-run `git check-attr export-ignore -- "<path>"` before assuming the mechanism itself
# is broken -- it is very likely this exact whitespace-parsing trap again.
#
# Internal planning / financial / analyst-process documents — never shipped to a
# stranger's clone:
"Revenue forecast.txt" export-ignore
